meta {
  name: position the browser pane
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/browser/viewport
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "x": 320,
    "y": 96,
    "width": 900,
    "height": 620,
    "visible": true,
    "sessionId": "{{sessionId}}"
  }
}

docs {
  The rectangle the web UI's browser segment reserved, in window CSS pixels; the server forwards it down the shell's control channel to place the native pane. 204 either way — with no shell attached the report is accepted and dropped, so the page never stalls.
  
  Body: Viewport(int x, int y, int width, int height, boolean visible, String sessionId) — sessionId names whose browser belongs in the rectangle; null/blank before a session exists.
  
  Side effect: Repositions (or hides) the native browser pane over the desktop window while a shell is attached.
  
  Fence: host+origin — Loopback fence plus Origin check: an Origin header, if present, must be loopback. REST clients send no Origin header, which counts as safe — requests pass. A manually added foreign Origin answers a blank 404.
  
  Writes state: no
}
